  "caption": {
    "text": "French AI lab Mistral put Mistral Large 4, nicknamed Le Chonk, into public preview on Oct 6 and is pitching it for cyber defense.\n\nThe weights, the trained parameters that let others modify and self-host a model, are due at the end of October, so it is not open-weight yet. The license terms are unknown until then. Mistral says it is the best open-weights model from the US or Europe. On the independent Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index it scores 38, well behind the leader, Claude Opus 5.5 (Max), at 58.\n\nMistral's argument is that closed models can block legitimate defensive work or be withdrawn mid-incident. It says Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra score near zero on a vulnerability test because they refuse the task. That claim comes only from Mistral, and the-decoder.com notes the test measures provider policy as much as model capability.\n\nWould you rather run your own security model or use one from a closed provider?\n\nFollow Helios Group to catch the end-of-October weights release and the license terms.\n\nSource: the-decoder.com, Wired, TechCrunch, CNBC, Artificial Analysis (LinkedIn) and Mistral on X, all October 6, 2026.",
    "facts": [
      "F1",
      "F2",
      "F4",
      "F6",
      "F8",
      "F10",
      "F14"
    ]
  },
  "edit_notes": [
    "Cover leads with Mistral (well_known true). 1T size is Mistral's figure (F3 claim_by Mistral); docs say 1.05T, so the number stays off body slides and only the cover uses '1T'.",
    "Skipped quote slides: every quote is single-source, and the slides carry the same points through sourced facts.",
    "Slide 4 shows the disagreement between Mistral's 'US or Europe' claim and Artificial Analysis's 'outside US and China' claim, rather than picking one.",
    "Slide 6 is the single background slide (B2). It names the June restrictions and the Hugging Face case, sourced to Wired and CNBC, and does not claim they caused Mistral's pitch.",
    "Split stat on slide 7 uses only independent Artificial Analysis numbers. Mistral's near-zero refusal claim is kept separate on slide 8 with attribution and the-decoder.com's caveat.",
    "Left out the conflicting numbers: GPU count (3,800 vs 4,000), context window (1M vs 512k) and the Series D figures. Also left out pricing, because the launch discount is unclear.",
    "Left out Mistral's company-reported 93.3% Lakera result and the coding scores, to keep the story on the cyber pitch and the gap to the frontier.",
    "The caption glosses open-weight using the CNBC TERMS entry. It carries the license-unknown point (F14) because the slides had no room for it."
  ]
}

